+
+For the fixed-band turbulence diagnostic, the RMS is formed over vertices without volume weighting and divided by the maximum reference field magnitude over the same set. Figure~\ref{fig:rans-eddy} displays this metric for eddy viscosity, avoiding comparison of unlike normalizations for $k$, $\omega$, and $\widetilde\nu$. In particular, the large wall value of $\omega$ can make a band-maximum-normalized discrepancy small without bounding its relative value everywhere. The archived surface distributions and endpoint tables provide $C_p$, streamwise $C_f$, and $y^+$ differences; the minimum streamwise $C_f$ near the nose is not used alone to diagnose separation.
